<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch10-section-barotropy-baroclinity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Barotropy and baroclinity</title>

  <p>
    A <term>barotropic</term> fluid is one in which density <m>\rho</m> depends only on pressure <m>p</m>,
    i.e., <m>\rho = \rho(p)</m>.
    Consider this restriction alongside the ideal gas law:
    Since <m>\rho</m> depends only on <m>p</m>,
    the ideal gas law implies that temperature <m>T</m> also depends only on <m>p</m>,
    i.e., <m>T = T(p)</m>.
    Consequently, isobaric, <term>isopycnic</term> (constant density), and isothermal surfaces
    are parallel within a barotropic fluid,
    as shown in <xref ref="ch10-barotropic" text="type-global" /> below.
    In other words,
    isobaric surfaces also are ispycnic and isothermal surfaces.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="ch10-barotropic">
    <caption>
        Stratification of isobaric (black), isopycnic (aqua), and isothermal (dashed red) surfaces
        within a barotropic fluid.
        The surfaces are parallel and tilt upward where the fluid is warmer.
        Figure and caption modified from image vectorised by User:Sushant savla from the work by Reas at German Wikipedia,
        via <url href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barotrope.svg">Wikimedia Commons</url>
        under <url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</url>.
    </caption>
    <image source="images/ch10-barotropic.png" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
            Rectangular prism volume of barotropic fluid with parallel black, aqua, and dashed red surfaces
            representing isobaric, isopycnic, and isothermal surfaces, respectively.
            The surfaces tilt upward where the fluid is warmer.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    It follows that in a barotropic atmosphere,
    <m>T</m> does not vary along isobaric surfaces,
    i.e., <m>\nabla_p T = \vec{0}</m>.
    Consequently,
    <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-balance-vector" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-balance-y" text="type-global" /> require that
    the geostrophic wind is not vertically sheared in a barotropic atmosphere,
    i.e., it is constant with height and <m>-\dfrac{\partial \vec{v}_g}{\partial p} = \vec{0}</m>.
    This means the thermal wind also is zero: <m>\vec{v}_T = \vec{0}</m>.
    So in a barotropic atmosphere,
    the geostrophic wind can vary with horizontal position and time but not with height.
    In other words,
    at a given moment in time,
    the geostrophic wind is constant in the vertical over any location of interest within a barotropic atmosphere.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <term>baroclinic</term> fluid is one in which <m>\rho</m> depends on both <m>p</m> and <m>T</m>,
    i.e., <m>\rho = \rho(p,T)</m>.
    Consider this statement alongside the ideal gas law:
    Since <m>\rho</m> depends on both <m>p</m> and <m>T</m>,
    the ideal gas law implies that <m>T</m> depends on both <m>p</m> and <m>\rho</m>,
    i.e., <m>T = T(p,\rho)</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is a more complicated situation than found in the barotropic fluid,
    and it means that isobaric, isopycnic, and isothermal surfaces are not parallel in a baroclinic fluid,
    an example of which is shown in <xref ref="ch10-baroclinic" text="type-global" /> below.
    Consequently,
    in a baroclinic atmosphere,
    temperature can vary along isobaric surfaces,
    and so there is no restriction on the vertical shear of the geostrophic wind and the thermal wind:
    The geostrophic wind can vary with horizontal position and time as well as with height
    as long as temperature gradients are present along isobaric surfaces.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="ch10-baroclinic">
    <caption>
        Possible stratification of isobaric (black), isopycnic (aqua), and isothermal (dashed red) surfaces
        within a baroclinic fluid.
        The surfaces are not parallel,
        and the isopycnic surfaces tilt downward where the fluid is warmer,
        indicating lower density in the warmer part of the fluid.
        Figure and caption modified from image vectorised by User:Sushant savla from the work by Reas at German Wikipedia,
        via <url href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baroclinic_fluid.png">Wikimedia Commons</url>
        under <url href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/">CC BY-SA 3.0</url>.
    </caption>
    <image source="images/ch10-baroclinic.png" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
            Rectangular prism volume of barotropic fluid with parallel black, aqua, and dashed red surfaces
            representing isobaric, isopycnic, and isothermal surfaces, respectively.
            The surfaces are not parallel,
            and the isopycnic surfaces tilt downward where the fluid is warmer.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    We can determine where the atmosphere is barotropic,
    approximately barotropic,
    or baroclinic using isobaric maps with isotherms contoured on them.
  </p>

  <p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Where the atmosphere is barotropic,
                isotherms will not be drawn on the map since temperature is constant on the isobaric surface.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Where the atmosphere is approximately barotropic,
                few isotherms will be drawn on the map,
                and they will be spaced relatively far apart from each other.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Where the atmosphere is baroclinic,
                many isotherms will be drawn on the map since temperature varies across the isobaric surface,
                and they will be spaced relatively close to each other.
                An example of this case is shown in <xref ref="ch10-baroclinic-map" text="type-global" /> below.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    Several isobaric maps for different pressure levels can be compared to determine the depth over which the atmosphere is barotropic,
    approximately barotropic,
    or baroclinic.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="ch10-baroclinic-map">
    <caption>
        850 hPa map with contoured isohypses of geopotential height (solid black; m),
        isotherms (dashed red for temperatures above 0°C, dashed blue for temperatures at and below 0°C),
        and isodrosotherms (dotted green for dewpoints at or above 0°C, shaded green for dewpoints at or above 6°C).
        Station models and pressure centers are annotated.
        Map courtesy of <url href="https://weather.cod.edu/">College of DuPage Weather Lab</url>.
    </caption>
    <image source="images/ch10-baroclinic-map.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
            850 hPa map with many closely spaced isotherms,
            indicating baroclinity of the atmosphere at this pressure level.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

</section>